The problem stated is the **Littlewood conjecture**: whether for all $\alpha, \beta \in \mathbb{R}$,

$$\liminf_{n\to\infty} n\,\|n\alpha\|\,\|n\beta\| = 0,$$

where $\|x\| = \operatorname{distToNearestInt}(x)$ denotes the distance to the nearest integer.

This is a famous **open problem** in Diophantine approximation, posed by John Edensor Littlewood around 1930. Despite substantial work — including the breakthrough of Einsiedler, Katok, and Lindenstrauss (2006), who used measure rigidity of higher-rank diagonal actions on homogeneous spaces to show that the set of exceptional $(\alpha,\beta)$ pairs (if any exist) has Hausdorff dimension zero — the conjecture remains **unresolved** in full generality. No counterexample is known, and no complete proof exists.

As a mathematical problem solver I am not able to produce a proof of an open conjecture that the mathematical community has not yet settled. I therefore cannot solve this problem.
